Flue-Gharana
Ingredients: BREAKBEAT GARAGE & GRIME + INDIAN RAGA + MELANCHOLIC, NOSTALGIC & SAD + Rhythm Electric Guitar + 2020s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Invented by deep-level miners trapped two kilometers underground who kept time by drumming on pressurized ventilation pipes.

Flue-Gharana is the acoustic shockwave of subterranean survival: a desolate, hyper-syncopated music where 138-BPM UK garage swing and bruising grime basslines collide with the microtonal grief of classical Indian ragas. Hollow, metallic pipe-drumming provides an erratic, breath-stealing groove, slashed open by weeping electric rhythm guitar skanks and long, mournful vocal glides that reverberate through miles of industrial tubing.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 136–142 BPM. The rhythmic chassis fuses classic 2-step garage skip and aggressive grime square-wave syncopations with North Indian tala cycles (notably 7-beat Rupak and 10-beat Jhaptal), driven by the resonant, clanging transients of hollow metal pipes.
- Harmonic Palette: Steeped in the heavy, melancholic dawn and twilight ragas—principally Raga Todi (with its weeping flat seconds and sharp fourths) and Raga Bhairav. Chordal structures rely on unresolved minor 9ths and detuned modal intervals that evoke bottomless physical isolation.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Acoustic Ducted Impulse Response (ADIR): Reverb tanks constructed exclusively from impulse recordings of kilometer-long corrugated air shafts, imparting a claustrophobic, ringing resonance.
  2. Gharana-Skank Gating: Rhythm electric guitar tracks heavily sidechained to erratic pipe-percussion hits, creating a frantic, stuttering chop that slides dynamically between dry garage skank and microtonal wah-pedal weeping.
  3. Barometric Pitch-Drift: The master tape speed and sub-bass drop a quarter-tone during bass drops to recreate the sudden, inner-ear pop of plummeting atmospheric pressure in deep mine shafts.
- Dynamics: Tracks commence in an aching, ambient Alap phase characterized by solo guitar feedback and distant airflow, abruptly fractured by sudden, jagged breakbeats and crushing 808 subs, before collapsing into hollow, sobbing pipe-taps.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born within the potash and tungsten boreholes deep beneath North Yorkshire and the Khewra salt shafts, Flue-Gharana was pioneered by second-generation British-South Asian drill-fitters and veteran Northern miners working under extreme depths. During mandatory air-choke intervals in sweltering shafts two kilometers beneath sea level, crews used twelve-pound spanners to beat complex tihais onto the galvanized 36-inch air mains, shouting improvised grime bars through respirator diaphragms to stay conscious. When brought to the surface in the mid-2020s, the style evolved into an intense rave subculture held in decommissioned grain silos and flooded dry docks. Followers wear frayed high-visibility boiler suits tailored into flowing kurtas, steel-toe boots strung with brass ghungroo ankle bells that rattle with every step, and communicate in a heavy hybrid patois known as Vent-Boli, which merges pirate-radio slang with classical Urdu and Punjabi musical nomenclature.

Typical Instruments
- Percussion: Struck 36-inch galvanized ventilation ducts, pneumatic valve pops, sampled mining spanners, 808 sub-kicks, and low-tuned acoustic dholak.
- Strings & Leads: Fender Telecaster (rhythm electric guitar, heavily featured for syncopated, chorus-drenched skanks and microtonal meend slides), electric sitar, bowed scrap-metal rebab.
- Synthesizers & Electronics: Novation Bass Station II (dirty square-wave grime basses), granular samplers loaded with underground turbine noise, analogue tape-flangers.
- Vocals: Half-sung khyal ornamentations alternating with breathless, staccato grime MCing filtered through vintage respirator masks.

For Fans Of: Burial, Nitin Sawhney, Dizzee Rascal (early Boy in da Corner era), Susheela Raman

